======================================================================================================================
L62 -- the GROUP channel: can existing measurements decide L57's nonlocal functional?
======================================================================================================================
    a0 footings: canonical 9.3619e-11 m/s^2, alt 1.1279e-10 m/s^2
    the observable: a metric slip shows up as  M_lens / M_dynamical  != 1  at group scale.
    L57 E3's prediction at its own working point (kernel n4, l_rms = 1 Mpc): 20 - 45 per cent.
    cosmic mean baryon density (Planck h, Omega_b = 0.049): 4.181e-28 kg/m^3

======================================================================================================================
PART A -- CONTROLS.  If one of these fails, the lane stops.
======================================================================================================================

  A1 -- CONTROL: L57's group numbers, its working point and its floor law, read back from
       L57_nonlocal_functional.out (not from the .md, and not from memory).
      L57 E3: 20 Lovisari 2015 groups, response 2.147e-01 - 4.504e-01 (median 3.078e-01)
      L57 working point: kernel n4 (elliptic order n = 4), l_rms = 1000 kpc
      L57 C5a floor at R_max = 100 kpc: 2.440e-04  (the (R_p/r_cl)^3 floor)
  [PASS] A1 [control] L57's group prediction, working point and floor are read back verbatim from its own output and are the 20-45% band this lane has to confront   (20 groups, 21% - 45% (median 31%), kernel n4 at l_rms = 1000 kpc)

  A2 -- CONTROL: the smoothing machinery, built independently in this file.  The elliptic operator
       (1 - l^2 D^2)^-n has the Whittle-Matern Green's function
           K_n(r) = m^3 2^(1-v) / [ (4 pi)^(3/2) Gamma(n) ] (m r)^v K_v(m r),   v = n - 3/2, m = 1/l,
       and the Gaussian e^(l^2 D^2 / 2) is (2 pi l^2)^(-3/2) exp(-r^2/2l^2).  For a spherically symmetric
       source the convolution collapses to one radial integral,
           U(R) = (2 pi / R) INT dr' r' rho(r') [ Phi_K(R+r') - Phi_K(|R-r'|) ],  Phi_K(x) = INT_0^x t K(t) dt.
       Three exact facts are checked: normalisation, the uniform fixed point, and a compact source
       returning M K(R).  The rms radius must come out as l sqrt(6n) (l sqrt(3) for the Gaussian).

      kernel    int K d^3r      rms/l  (expected)
      gauss     1.00000000    1.732   ( 1.732)
      n1        1.00000000    2.449   ( 2.449)
      n2        1.00000000    3.464   ( 3.464)
      n3        1.00000000    4.243   ( 4.243)
      n4        1.00000000    4.899   ( 4.899)
      n6        1.00000000    6.000   ( 6.000)
      gauss     uniform fixed point 1.000007   point source / M K(r) 1.00000, 1.00000
      n1        uniform fixed point 1.000012   point source / M K(r) 1.00000, 1.00000
      n2        uniform fixed point 1.000007   point source / M K(r) 1.00000, 1.00000
      n3        uniform fixed point 1.000007   point source / M K(r) 1.00000, 1.00000
      n4        uniform fixed point 1.000007   point source / M K(r) 1.00000, 1.00000
      n6        uniform fixed point 1.000007   point source / M K(r) 1.00000, 1.00000
  [PASS] A2 [control] the smoothing machinery built in this file is exact: every kernel is normalised, has rms radius l sqrt(6n), leaves a uniform density exactly where it is, and returns M K(R) for a compact source   (worst normalisation error 5.00e-15, worst rms error 2.22e-16, worst uniform error 1.23e-05, worst point-source error 4.34e-06)

  A3 -- CONTROL: L57's floor law, re-derived here.  For a source COMPACT against l the smoothed trigger is
       U(R) = M K(R), so the trigger gradient per unit of the object's own acceleration is
           ratio = |dU/dR| / (G M / R^2) = |K'(R)| R^2 / G,
       and the mass cancels exactly.  The small-r behaviour of K' therefore fixes the whole law:
           K'(r) ~ r^(q-2)  =>  ratio ~ R^q  =>  S = ratio_obj/ratio_cl ~ (R_p/r_cl)^q.
       Measured here on this lane's own kernels, with no reference to L57's numbers.

      gauss   : K'(r) ~ r^+1.000  =>  q = 3.000   |   full convolution of a compact source: ratio(R_p) ~ R_p^3.001
      n1      : K'(r) ~ r^-2.000  =>  q = -0.000   |   full convolution of a compact source: ratio(R_p) ~ R_p^-0.001
      n2      : K'(r) ~ r^-0.008  =>  q = 1.992   |   full convolution of a compact source: ratio(R_p) ~ R_p^1.975
      n3      : K'(r) ~ r^+0.992  =>  q = 2.992   |   full convolution of a compact source: ratio(R_p) ~ R_p^2.975
      n4      : K'(r) ~ r^+1.000  =>  q = 3.000   |   full convolution of a compact source: ratio(R_p) ~ R_p^3.006
      n6      : K'(r) ~ r^+1.000  =>  q = 3.000   |   full convolution of a compact source: ratio(R_p) ~ R_p^3.017
  [PASS] A3 [control] the suppression law reproduces: q = 0 for n = 1, q = 2 for n = 2, and q = 3 -- the CUBE of the scale ratio -- for every n >= 3 and for the Gaussian, so no kernel order and no length beats (R_p/r_cl)^3.  Checked twice: from K'(r) analytically, and end-to-end through the full convolution of a compact source   (q = gauss 3.00, n1 -0.00, n2 1.99, n3 2.99, n4 3.00, n6 3.00; the end-to-end convolution exponents agree to 0.017)

      X-COP profiles read from real_research/data/xcop for the five clusters with published weak lensing

  A4 -- CONTROL: the 9-sigma cluster shear-SHAPE failure, recomputed here from the X-COP baryons
       and the Herbonnet 2020 weak-lensing NFWs, with this file's own projection integrals.
      canonical: framework log-slope -0.307, measured -0.823, difference +0.516 +/- 0.059  (8.8 sigma)
      alt      : framework log-slope -0.302, measured -0.823, difference +0.521 +/- 0.058  (8.9 sigma)
  [PASS] A4 [control] the 9-sigma cluster shear-shape failure reproduces independently (L51 C2 / L56 A3 / L57 A2: +0.516 +/- 0.058 canonical, +0.521 +/- 0.058 alt)   (canonical +0.516 +/- 0.059 (8.8 sigma), alt +0.521 +/- 0.058 (8.9 sigma))

  A5 -- CONTROL: the measured cluster lensing-to-dynamical mass ratio, recomputed.
